The nationwide general strike against Staal's authoritarian regime has brought the country to a standstill. The chancellor could unleash the Commonwealth Army and <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span> paramilitaries to brutally crush the protests—but for now, that remains a last resort. Instead, Staal has chosen to open negotiations with the opposition.

Meanwhile, in defiance of the indefinite adjournment of the Assembly, the <span style="color: #000000;">rep</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">ubl</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ican</span> parties have banded together to unofficially reconvene the chamber at a separate location, openly challenging federal authority.

However, perhaps we have overestimated our strength—strikes rarely last with unemployment so high, especially with Staal’s sympathizers embedded within the unions themselves.

- @z_break
- @schleicher_cabinet_13_pre_pre

@spd_lose
title: Our protests are ineffective.
view-if: marcher_lose
on-arrival: hammerstein_win = 1

The general strike proves short-lived. An endless supply of unemployed strikebreakers and Staal's own sympathizers within the unions render it largely ineffective. Protesters are shot, civilians killed. The the Banners is no match for the Commonwealth Army.

Seizing the moment, Staal expands his crackdown, accusing the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> of conspiring with the <span style="color: #700000;">**Communists**</span> to incite revolution.

the Commonwealth is lost.

- @game_over

@z_break
